Fall back to UTC when TZ is unset and /etc/localtime does not exist.

Lean's TZdb reads /etc/localtime unconditionally when TZ is unset. No Wolfi
package installs that file, so every local-time query fails, and the language
server's logging module makes one on startup: `lean --server` answers
`initialize` and then dies with "Watchdog error: no such file or directory
(error code: 2) file: /etc/localtime". The C library falls back to UTC in this
situation; this patch does the same, only for the implicit default path, so an
explicit TZ that cannot be resolved still fails loudly.

Upstream: https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/issues/12869 (open) and the
proposed fix https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/10509, which falls back
to the tzdata "UTC" file; this patch uses the built-in fixed-offset rules so
the fallback needs no files at all.

--- a/src/Std/Time/Zoned/Database/TZdb.lean
+++ b/src/Std/Time/Zoned/Database/TZdb.lean
@@ -160,9 +160,13 @@
 /--
 Retrieves the timezone rules for the local timezone, re-reading the `TZ` and `TZDIR`
 environment variables on each call so that runtime changes are reflected immediately.
+Falls back to UTC when `TZ` is unset and the system has no `/etc/localtime`, as the C
+library does, instead of failing every local-time query (leanprover/lean4#12869).
 -/
 def getLocalZoneRules (db : TZdb) : IO ZoneRules := do
   let path ← resolveLocalPath (← db.resolveZonesPaths)
+  if path.toString == "/etc/localtime" && !(← path.pathExists) then
+    return ZoneRules.UTC
   localRules path
 
 /--
